{
  "header": [
    "UNALLOCATED — SHOULD — a policy entry keyed by a Base58 peer-id is rewritten to the hex identity-hash key once that peer connects.",
    "",
    "Batch 4. §6.9a.1 / §6.2 first-contact canonicalization. A different \"canonical\" from §1.5's: the KEY of the",
    "policy path moves from peer-id to identity hash. A SHOULD with no §9.2 row, and §6.2 calls it the rule-3",
    "discipline, which is a MUST (F35)."
  ],
  "id_status": "unallocated",
  "under": "",
  "title": "After a peer whose policy entry exists only at system/capability/policy/{Base58 peer-id} authenticates, an equivalent entry exists at policy/{hex identity hash} and the Base58-keyed entry does not",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §6.9a.1 ('First-contact canonicalization (per v7.64 §2.3, SHOULD): when a Base58-form policy entry matches, the peer SHOULD canonicalize — write an equivalent hex-form entry at the identity-hash path carrying the same `(scope, bounds)`, then delete the Base58-form entry.')",
  "spec_also": [
    "§6.9a.1 ('Canonicalization timing is impl-defined ... Conformance tests the eventual canonicalized state, not the precise timing.')",
    "§6.2 ('on first contact the implementation SHOULD canonicalize the entry to hex in place, per the §3.6 rule-3 lazy-canonicalization discipline') — §3.6 rule 3 is MUST, F35",
    "§6.2 resolution order (hex, then Base58, then default)",
    "§9.1 'Policy-derived authenticate grant' (ECP-R63) — the resolution, not this rewrite",
    "§6.2 writing policy entries (raw `tree:put` 'skips' configure's normalization invariants)",
    "§6.9a.0 ('The validator harness MUST NOT enumerate artifact paths') — scoped to seed artifacts; see reading"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "SHOULD",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F35, F37",
  "reading": [
    "THE LEVEL IS THE KEYWORD, TWICE. §6.9a.1 \"SHOULD canonicalize\"; §6.2 \"SHOULD canonicalize the entry to hex in",
    "place\". §6.2 then says it is \"per the §3.6 rule-3 lazy-canonicalization discipline\" — and rule 3 is a MUST. The",
    "two are different acts on different objects (rule 3 rewrites a peer reference INSIDE a pattern to canonical",
    "Base58; this rewrites a policy path KEY from Base58 to hex), so the cross-reference borrows a discipline, not a",
    "level. Authored at SHOULD from the two sentences that state this act; the borrowed MUST is routed (F35).",
    "",
    "\"EVENTUAL\", AND WHAT WE DECLARE. \"Timing is impl-defined ... impls MAY defer canonicalization to first",
    "cap-match at request-time. Both timings satisfy\". Policy is consulted at authenticate (§6.9a) and at",
    "`system/capability:request` (§6.2) — not on a tree read. So the observation is taken after the handshake AND after",
    "one `capability:request` by K (the latest trigger the text names), then after `observation_window`. ⚠ Corrected",
    "after review: the first draft sent a `tree:get`, which consults no policy, and would have WARNed a peer deferring to",
    "request-time. Declared, never assumed.",
    "",
    "WRITTEN RAW, AND WHY (found by review). An Ed25519 `C(K)` carries the key, so a `configure` applying §6.2's",
    "\"normalization invariants\" may write the hex key at once. That is not forbidden, and it would make the control",
    "(J's entry still Base58, no hex) fail a conformant peer. Both entries are therefore written with a raw",
    "`system/tree:put`, which §6.2 says \"skips those\", and read back before any contact: if a hex entry exists already,",
    "the file is void (recorded) rather than scored.",
    "",
    "§6.9a.0'S \"MUST NOT ENUMERATE ARTIFACT PATHS\". That sentence is about the SEED policy's two artifact shapes, which",
    "are \"impl-private per §9.4\". The entries here are written by the suite at the path §6.2 and §6.9a.1 define, and",
    "§6.9a.1 itself says \"Conformance tests the eventual canonicalized state\" of exactly these keys. Read back, not",
    "enumerated.",
    "",
    "WHICH Base58 FORM. The key is written in canonical form `C(K)`. Writing `S(K)` would stack this file on F35's",
    "open question (a non-canonical form in a stored tree path, which §1.4 calls non-conformant) and on F31.",
    "",
    "SCORED AS A WARN. A SHOULD is never a gate (DESIGN §3)."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [
      "system/tree: put, get on system/capability/policy/*"
    ],
    "identities": [
      "caller",
      "K (never connected before the put)",
      "J (never connected)"
    ],
    "handlers": [
      "system/capability with the request operation"
    ],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy",
      "observation_window"
    ]
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "rewritten",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "the caller raw-puts policy/{C(K)} with a distinctive grant (covering one system/capability:request); reads back both keys; K authenticates with C(K) and sends one capability:request; wait observation_window",
      "why": "§6.9a.1's SHOULD, at the eventual state the section says conformance tests.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "operator"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "put_base58_K",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "put",
          "note": "system/capability/policy/{C(K)} := policy-entry {peer_pattern: C(K), grants: [<distinctive, includes system/capability request>]}"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "pre_contact_hex_K",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "get",
          "note": "system/capability/policy/{lowercase hex of H(K)} — must be 404, or the file is void"
        },
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "as_K",
          "identity": "K"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "K_capability_request",
          "uri": "system/capability",
          "operation": "request",
          "note": "a request within the distinctive grant — the request-time cap-match §6.9a.1 names"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "read_hex_key",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "get",
          "note": "after observation_window: system/capability/policy/{lowercase hex of H(K)}"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "read_base58_key",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "get",
          "note": "system/capability/policy/{C(K)}"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "read_hex_key",
          "equals": 200,
          "when": "pre_contact_hex_K.status == 404"
        },
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "read_base58_key",
          "equals": 404,
          "when": "pre_contact_hex_K.status == 404"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the same raw put for a key J that never connects; both of J's keys read after the same window",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY. J's Base58 entry must still be there and no hex entry for J may exist: proves the rewrite is triggered by CONTACT, and that the main arm's 404 is not a peer that discards Base58-keyed entries (which would also make ECP-R63's Base58 resolution step unreachable). Raw put, so no configure-time normalization can make a conformant peer fail it.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "put_base58_J",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "put",
          "note": "system/capability/policy/{C(J)} := policy-entry {peer_pattern: C(J), grants: [<the same>]}"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "read_base58_key_J",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "get",
          "note": "after K's contact and observation_window: system/capability/policy/{C(J)}"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "read_hex_key_J",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "get",
          "note": "system/capability/policy/{lowercase hex of H(J)}"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "read_base58_key_J",
          "equals": 200
        },
        {
          "kind": "status",
          "capture": "read_hex_key_J",
          "equals": 404
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that resolves the Base58 entry and never rewrites it",
      "expect": "read_base58_key is 200 and read_hex_key is 404 indefinitely",
      "why": "a SHOULD, so a WARN: the entry keeps working via the Base58 resolution step, but identity-keyed tooling (quorum, controller, operator identities 'work in hex form only', §6.9a.1) never sees it."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "",
    "oracle_note": "no `peer_canonicalization` reference check cites §6.9a.1. The proposal names a v7.64-era `policy_dual_form` category (POL-DF-2) as the legacy site; not looked up."
  }
}
